I'd prefer for this type to be:

```ts
export type ParseResult = {
  /**
   * The diagram type, e.g. 'flowchart', 'sequence', etc.
   */
  diagramType: string;
};
```

And then we change the type for `parse()` to (may need to modify this slightly to get TypeScript's function overloading working):

```ts
async function parse(text: string, parseOptions?: {supressErrors: false}): Promise<ParseResult>;
async function parse(text: string, parseOptions?: ParseOptions): Promise<ParseResult | false> {
```

That way TypeScript will know that this function never returns `false` unless `{supressErrors: true}` is set.

It will also be much easier for users (and us) to declare a variable to be of type `ParseResult`!

```suggestion
export type ParseResult =
  | {
      isValid: true;
      /**
       * The diagram type, e.g. 'flowchart', 'sequence', etc.
       */
      diagramType: string;
    }
  | false;
```

We could have something like this instead, so that `if(await mermaid.parse(code))` will still fail.
